      "math_academy_id": "q-290907",
      "problem": "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}5.711 & 3.866 & -3.400 \\\\ 3.580 & -0.216 & -3.789 \\\\ -0.507 & -5.693 & -3.727 \\\\ 1.640 & -0.269 & 0.705 \\\\ -0.225 & -4.111 & -2.130\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe wish to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset while still preserving as much variance as possible.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning that walks through the process of reducing the dimensionality of the dataset.\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is \n{{field-1}} \nwith the \n{{field-2}} \nvariance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction in feature space \n{{field-3}} \nto the first\n{{field-4}}. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to {{field-5}}.\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{std}}=[\\begin{aligned}1.40 & 1.37 & -0.49 \\\\ 0.59 & 0.29 & -0.70 \\\\ -0.97 & -1.18 & -0.66 \\\\ -0.15 & 0.27 & 1.67 \\\\ -0.86 & -0.75 & 0.18\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.713 & 0.123 & -0.47 & 0.506 & 0.013 \\\\ -0.25 & 0.321 & 0.709 & 0.213 & 0.535 \\\\ 0.509 & 0.422 & 0.046 & 0.667 & -0.342 \\\\ 0.042 & -0.839 & 0.241 & 0.487 & 0.01 \\\\ 0.41 & -0.027 & -0.465 & 0.133 & 0.773\\end{aligned}], \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.809 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.020 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.114 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.711 & 0.051 & 0.701 \\\\ -0.693 & -0.218 & -0.687 \\\\ 0.118 & -0.975 & 0.191\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n{{field-6}}.\n\nThe fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n{{field-7}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-8}}.\n\nWriting ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ for the columns of $V$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3},{u}_{4},{u}_{5}$ for the columns of $U$, the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{red}}={{field-9}}.\n$$",
